WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:16.160
Music

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.800
[Bombs exploding and artillery fire sounds]

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:25.900
[Narrator:] Summer, 1945, and the war is one million men old.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:32.300
[Machine gun fire in bursts]

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:41.566
[Dramatic music]

00:00:41.566 --> 00:00:48.499
[Speaker 1:]  Hang on kid. Keep your eyes shut tight and drink.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:52.300
They're moving you back now.  Don't give up, just hang on.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:57.166
The road back is bumpy and maybe the pain blurs your eyes, but listen:

00:00:57.166 --> 00:01:01.099
The sound of battle grows dim.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:08.233
And now one question cuts clearly through the haze: Which man will you be?

00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:11.333
The one who gets hurt and dies?

00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:14.099
Or the one who gets hurt and lives?

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.300
When the dizziness stops, when the fog clears,

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.400
an army nurse will be at your side.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.600
A woman who has meant safety and comfort and home

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:32.400
to thousands of men before you, a woman who will mean all those things to you.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.400
A nurse brought another American's blood
to your side

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.540
to pour new strength into your veins.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:43.960
A nurse handed clamps to the surgeon and counted sponges.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.400
A nurse prepared and administered the anesthetic

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.333
and watched you constantly for any telltale change in your breathing

00:01:51.333 --> 00:01:55.466
or blood pressure.  All of them working with the same purpose:

00:01:55.466 --> 00:01:59.460
to ease the pain of war, to help save lives.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:08.960
[Music increases]

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.800
The preparation for the moment that would bring the Army nurse to your side

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.240
began months ago back home in the United States.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:17.880
It started with four weeks of basic training.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:26.100
In those days, perhaps the nurse wondered why she had to sit through seemingly endless classes

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.700
and submit to rigid discipline.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:32.633
Often while muscles ached and groaned,

00:02:32.633 --> 00:02:34.499
she may have wondered why it was necessary

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.840
to take those long hikes or grope her way through a gassed area.

00:02:38.866 --> 00:02:43.220
Yet, there were demands that would require of her perfect physical health and stamina,

00:02:43.233 --> 00:02:46.933
the strength to stand up under the rigors of combat nursing.

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:50.233
Now, when the four weeks of basic training are finished,

00:02:50.233 --> 00:02:54.333
the Army nurse is ready to serve, no matter where her assignment may take her.

00:02:54.333 --> 00:02:58.866
She might find herself stationed in a general hospital right here at home,

00:02:58.866 --> 00:03:02.999
or perhaps she'll find herself assigned to a mobile hospital unit overseas.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.333
After she arrives, she may have to help build the very hospital

00:03:06.333 --> 00:03:11.066
in which she is to work.  For the field hospital or the evacuation hospital,

00:03:11.066 --> 00:03:15.099
like a circus, must be able to pack up and move on at a moment's notice.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:19.200
Its primary function is to offer immediate surgical treatment

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.433
to the wounded, and that means following ever-changing battle lines.

00:03:23.433 --> 00:03:26.533
Everyone pitches in when a mobile hospital goes up:

00:03:26.533 --> 00:03:29.533
enlisted men, doctors, and nurses.

00:03:29.533 --> 00:03:33.499
Just one small instance where basic training pays off, those muscles

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:40.200
toughened and hardened during the four weeks of basic back home are equal to the job.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:45.533
In the field, the Army nurse lives roughly and works gently.

00:03:45.533 --> 00:03:49.333
There is no glamour, and her life is far from spectacular.

00:03:49.333 --> 00:03:54.666
She sleeps under hastily pitched canvas on a GI cot under GI blankets.

00:03:54.666 --> 00:03:59.699
She trains her mind to act as an alarm clock, because time is important.

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.460
A wasted moment may mean a wasted life.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:10.660
She lives a life completely stripped of luxuries, and yet,

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:15.480
she has asked for no more luxury than a patient's smile when the pain is eased.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:17.800
She eats regular GI rations,

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:21.733
the same as the rest of the Army does and often at irregular times.

00:04:21.733 --> 00:04:25.633
The hours are long and the demands never ending, and as a result,

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:29.520
the nurse has learned to make use of every moment of her off duty time.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:37.800
A GI helmet may not be exactly what she'd choose to wear to her

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:42.566
kid sister's wedding, but it makes up for its lack of style by its versatility.

00:04:42.566 --> 00:04:53.432
It's a beauty parlor, laundry, cooking pot, wash basin, and anything else you

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:57.340
might wish to call it, a little community in size seven.

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:01.000
She may spend some of her time writing letters,

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.733
not the "having a wonderful time, wish you were here" kind,

00:05:04.733 --> 00:05:09.833
but letters filled with all the drama of her days, with stories of the courage

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.080
and spirit of the men over whom she watches.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.560
She may devote a few moments to prayer,

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:22.600
others to lounging about talking of home.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:26.100
She may long to wear the evening dress sent from home,

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.800
but probably only gets the chance to talk about it.

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:33.766
Usually, she wears olive drab or battle gray and is battle-ready throughout the day,

00:05:33.766 --> 00:05:38.532
on duty or off.  Her uniform at all times is her badge of service.

00:05:38.533 --> 00:05:42.099
But however she spends her time, she always wants to return to the

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:45.033
hospital where the wounded are fighting for their lives.

00:05:45.033 --> 00:05:49.099
For first and foremost, and at all times, she is a nurse,

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.633
offering professional and skilled care

00:05:51.633 --> 00:05:56.666
to the sick and wounded.  A nurse first, a woman second, and an officer third

00:05:56.666 --> 00:06:01.099
might well serve as the slogan for every member of the nurses corps.

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:04.766
Complete recovery of the patient depends not only upon the use of drugs,

00:06:04.766 --> 00:06:09.666
but on the skill with which they are administered and the care that follows.

00:06:09.666 --> 00:06:13.366
The nurse must be capable of recognizing at once any symptoms

00:06:13.366 --> 00:06:16.032
in her patients which demand immediate treatment,

00:06:16.033 --> 00:06:18.766
because if serious consequences are to be avoided,

00:06:18.766 --> 00:06:23.032
medical treatment must be on hand the moment any symptoms appear.

00:06:23.033 --> 00:06:27.966
Professionally skilled and capable, in her there is the

00:06:27.966 --> 00:06:33.699
tenderness of all women, of mother and sister and friend.

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:38.833
Her touch and voice lend encouragement, instill hope.

00:06:38.833 --> 00:06:41.966
It's the surgeon who saves a man's life.

00:06:41.966 --> 00:06:44.580
It's the nurse whose loving care helps him to live.

00:06:44.740 --> 00:07:01.500
[Music]

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:03.800
The crisis has passed.

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:05.966
The patients begin to sleep again.

00:07:05.966 --> 00:07:09.132
Now, the pain is just a bad memory.

00:07:09.133 --> 00:07:12.766
The field hospital is a stopover to give them immediate surgical treatment

00:07:12.766 --> 00:07:14.966
as soon as possible after they're wounded.

00:07:14.966 --> 00:07:19.966
[Music]

00:07:19.966 --> 00:07:22.332
[15th. Evacuation Hospital]

00:07:22.333 --> 00:07:27.399
The evacuation hospital is also just a stopover on the trip back through the medical chain.

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:31.733
Here, facilities for treatment are more complete than those of the field hospital.

00:07:31.733 --> 00:07:36.066
And here, too, the nurse plays an increasingly important part

00:07:36.066 --> 00:07:38.400
in the vital period of treatment and convalescence.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:41.660
After three or four days,

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:44.940
the patients are well enough for the trip back to the general hospital.

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:57.500
Trains will be waiting for them, hospital wards on wheels.

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:08.040
[Music]

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:12.733
Each train carries, in addition to medical officers
and enlisted technicians,

00:08:12.733 --> 00:08:17.099
four surgical nurses and two medical nurses, all of whom

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:21.066
are on duty from 7:00 AM to 9:00 PM

00:08:21.066 --> 00:08:22.980
and as far into the night as they are needed.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:30.300
When speed means life, evacuation may take place by plane.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:34.566
Because of these flying hospitals, men are alive today

00:08:34.566 --> 00:08:39.260
who otherwise would have died in the jungles of Burma or on the beaches of Normandy.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:42.333
These patients are thoroughly checked before the take off.

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:45.940
They will be watched over and checked again when the plane lands.

00:08:53.966 --> 00:08:57.332
While in the air, the flight nurse is in complete charge.

00:08:57.340 --> 00:09:03.380
She is ready to handle every emergency and does everything a doctor would have to do, except operate.

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:06.333
Air evacuation is difficult.

00:09:06.333 --> 00:09:11.066
It requires specialized skill and training, and the flight nurse must be prepared

00:09:11.066 --> 00:09:14.560
for the unexpected, for the next moment it may happen.

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:20.920
At the general hospital in the theater of operation,

00:09:20.933 --> 00:09:24.999
other skilled teams of surgeons and nurses stand ready to take over

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:26.740
where the frontline hospitals left off.

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:32.160
Whether a hospital is under canvas out in the field or in a solid structure in a city,

00:09:32.166 --> 00:09:40.032
it's routine, like the nurses routine, doesn't vary, and its principal aim remains fixed: to offer the best

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:42.540
possible medical care to the sick and wounded.

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:50.880
Nurses sterilize the operating equipment, the surgical gowns and gloves,

00:09:54.160 --> 00:10:00.320
prepare drugs, lay out the instruments the doctor will need for his next operation,

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:03.840
and keep accurate records of each patient's history and progress.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:11.333
While in a civilian hospital the ratio is one nurse to every three beds, in an army hospital,

00:10:11.333 --> 00:10:13.466
it's a great many times that number.

00:10:13.466 --> 00:10:18.732
To help lift the burden from the nurse's shoulders, enlisted technicians trained in special schools

00:10:18.733 --> 00:10:21.999
will be assigned to work under her direct supervision.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:26.266
One of her biggest jobs is to teach these technicians both in the classroom and

00:10:26.266 --> 00:10:27.720
in the hospital wards.

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:32.233
This means added responsibility for the nurse, for although she will

00:10:32.233 --> 00:10:34.766
have less personal contact with her patients,

00:10:34.766 --> 00:10:38.266
she must direct the activities of the personnel assigned to her

00:10:38.266 --> 00:10:41.820
so that the treatment and well-being of her patients is assured.

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:48.433
Relaxation and entertainment are an important part of medical treatment.

00:10:48.433 --> 00:10:52.333
To this general hospital overseas comes an all-star show,

00:10:52.333 --> 00:10:56.199
and the Army nurse shares the fun with her patients.

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:59.460
The sound of laughter from home often means more than medicine.

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:04.760
[Applause]

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:07.360
Later on, she may spend a few minutes with the star,

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:12.733
and there may be times when she herself is the star of the show,

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:15.720
like this nurse, the first woman to land on Bougainville.  [Bougainville Campaign]

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:23.460
The umpire calls batter up and the game is on: army versus army,

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:25.120
as the nurses play the WACs.

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:29.460
[Music]

00:11:29.466 --> 00:11:33.032
This infield may never play in the Polo Grounds,

00:11:33.033 --> 00:11:36.199
but maybe one of their patients will.

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:40.900
[Crowd cheering, applause]

00:11:41.780 --> 00:11:47.000
Off duty for a while, some nurses may choose to stroll through the streets and buildings of an ancient city.

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:51.000
Others may prefer a round of golf,

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:57.300
or a swim somewhere if the weather is right.

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:04.660
But it's always back to duty, back to the sick and wounded, back to the hospital,

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:07.820
around which the life of the Army nurse revolves.

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:11.460
Those patients scheduled for a trip back home

00:12:11.466 --> 00:12:15.266
for the final period of convalescence travel on a hospital ship,

00:12:15.266 --> 00:12:17.700
as fully equipped as a stationary hospital.

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:23.400
Now as before, the Army nurse stands by ready to administer to every need.

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:31.700
[Music]

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:34.900
Next stop, a debarkation hospital,

00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:48.933
[Music, "My Country 'Tis of Thee"]

00:12:48.933 --> 00:12:53.799
then a general hospital in the United States and complete recovery.

00:12:53.800 --> 00:12:59.260
And this is the end of the long chain of evacuation, the general hospital back home.

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:04.660
Here, the Army nurse works patiently with her hands, her head, and her heart.

00:13:04.660 --> 00:13:08.960
Here, she gently guides men back to the way of life they fought to protect.

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:12.460
Although she may have volunteered to serve overseas,

00:13:12.466 --> 00:13:19.299
although she may chafe at what she considers the inactivity of working in a hospital in the United States or Alaska or Panama,

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:24.520
the Army nurse soon learns that a battle line is wherever a soldier is stationed.

00:13:27.260 --> 00:13:31.520
What words of praise can measure up to these women, whose very lives

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:37.260
are given to nursing a wounded man, a million wounded men, back to life and health?

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:44.820
And yet, praise is offered.

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:48.980
Long hours of tireless service are remembered and recognized.

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:55.240
The Army nurse, decorated for bravery and valor, above and beyond the call of duty.

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:02.220
And these nurses imprisoned for three long years by the Japanese

00:14:02.233 --> 00:14:07.833
ask only that they be returned to duty, for they will never forget the faces of the American men

00:14:07.833 --> 00:14:12.666
tortured and killed by the enemy.  Wherever transports are taking our soldiers,

00:14:12.666 --> 00:14:14.799
they are also taking the Army nurse,

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:20.533
to work by day, by night on distant battlefields, to help make shattered bodies whole,

00:14:20.533 --> 00:14:23.666
to bring smiles to faces twisted with pain,

00:14:23.666 --> 00:14:29.966
to serve at the side of the man hurt by war, directly by shellfire or indirectly by disease.

00:14:29.966 --> 00:14:33.399
This is the Army nurse, USA,

00:14:33.400 --> 00:14:38.566
ready to serve anywhere, under any circumstances, in time of need.

00:14:38.566 --> 00:14:46.800
[Music]

